WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.380
SEAN LOPP: Thank you all for
taking some time out

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.630
of your day to be with us.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:13.430
Really looking forward to
talking about dashboard fatigue

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.080
and how to avoid it.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.560
And then just to reiterate what
Rob said, because it's always

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.460
our perennial question, the
recording will be available.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.960
And then we're going to be going
through some slides as well

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.940
as some code today.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.650
That's available
already if you want

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:30.260
to go to this link
to follow along.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.620
It's a GitHub repository
that has the slides as well

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.830
as the code.

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:38.580
So dashboard fatigue,
what are we talking about?

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.760
Before we can address
kind of that question,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.130
we actually need to take
a step back and think

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:48.510
for just a second about what
the goals of a data science team

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:49.830
really are.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.570
And at the end of
the day at RStudio,

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.450
we believe that the main
objective of a data science

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:58.560
team is to help organizations
and individuals make

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.130
better decisions that
are informed by data.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.180
And so that's a
really admirable goal,

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.500
but it comes with its
set of challenges.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.140
And one of the challenges
that we've seen time and time

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.790
again is that there's often
a gap between data scientists

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.575
and those stakeholders
they're hoping to inform.

00:01:17.575 --> 00:01:19.980
And that gap, a lot of
times, is the result

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.580
of different mediums.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.750
So data scientists can be
discovering insight, you're

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:30.150
writing code in R, Python,
whereas decision makers often

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:35.400
are spending their time either
in meetings or in their inbox

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.550
or perhaps within spreadsheets.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.870
And more and more,
they're doing all of that

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:45.480
if on the go as they
take care of 1,000 things

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.010
at once, often on their phone.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.550
And so for data scientists,
data science teams,

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:54.420
we have to find a way
to bridge that gap so

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.710
that our analysis can still
be engaging and inform

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.170
and ultimately, drive
those decisions.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.210
There's a few different
ways that we've

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.970
seen teams kind of close
this gap in practice.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:11.260
So one way that at
RStudio we're big fans of

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.980
is building dashboards.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.510
And so if packages like Shiny
on the R ecosystem or Dash,

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.420
for example, on the
Python ecosystem.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.579
You can create these
interactive dashboards

00:02:23.579 --> 00:02:26.890
that are more engaging
for those stakeholders.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.340
So they can explore
your analysis,

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.770
they can ask questions,
they can kind of

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.410
have an understanding
that then allows

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.460
them to make better decisions.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.300
So that's one of practical
and very popular way

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.370
to close that gap.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.110
Of course, there's
other ways, as well.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.970
A lot of time and
energy has been

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.000
spent explaining how
data scientists can put

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.830
their models into production.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.260
That often is in
the form of an API

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.050
so that other systems
and services can you

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.710
help take advantage
of those insights.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.300
And then sometimes,
we might resort

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.930
to doing things like modifying
PowerPoint presentations

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:07.110
or you're copying
and pasting results

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.370
in kind of more manual ways.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.990
So these are all different
ways that data science schemes

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.420
can bridge that
gap between mediums

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.950
to make their work more
accessible to decision makers.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:24.290
But what we've seen is that
sometimes, especially for--

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.550
there's really awesome
interesting and exciting

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.840
dashboards--

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.750
data science teams can fall
victims to their own success.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.240
So what do I mean by that?

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.280
Well, a few weeks
ago on Twitter,

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.150
Angela Bassa, who leads the
data science team at iRobot--

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.600
they make those robotic
vacuum cleaners--

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.070
she asked this
question on Twitter.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:48.150
She said, how do you keep track
of all of your dashboards?

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:51.080
This led to a really
interesting kind of exchange

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:55.380
and I want to show just a few
of the responses that she got.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.900
So one response
is that you could

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.350
go about making a dashboard
for the dashboards, so

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.510
kind of a meta dashboard.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:07.200
And this was actually the most
popular response to the thread.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.090
Very kind of data
science way of thinking

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.400
about solving this problem.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.663
Maybe I can use the
same hammer that I

00:04:14.663 --> 00:04:16.080
used to solve the
original problem

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.390
and just make more dashboards.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:22.140
Another answer, this one I
found pretty interesting,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:26.340
a lot of teams will create
an index of the work.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.380
Maybe that is a spreadsheet
with links to the dashboards,

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.463
maybe it's a page on like,
Confluence or SharePoint.

00:04:34.463 --> 00:04:35.880
And of course, the
challenge there

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.000
is that you have to
hope that you keep--

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.800
you remember keep it updated.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.570
And then I think this answer
was probably the most honest,

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:47.650
which is that a lot of times,
there's not a great response.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.690
That data science
teams can produce

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:51.400
all of these
interesting dashboards,

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:55.960
but there's no great way for
others to interact and keep

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.430
track of them.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.290
And ultimately,
this is a problem,

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:04.210
because it kind of impairs
our ability as data scientists

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.940
to close that gap and
inform those decisions.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.210
Often, what we'll see is
a really great dashboard

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:14.680
will be created and users
will engage with it once.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.730
But over time, in the coming
days, weeks, and months,

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:20.710
the dashboard falls
by the wayside,

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:23.110
especially if there's
many of these dashboards.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:27.600
It's just a pretty big burden
to ask for your decision makers

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.040
every day to go play
around with the dashboard.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.320
To keep track of
what's going on.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:33.760
So that creates a
problem for us--

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:36.280
is that if we just wait
for decision makers

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.710
to proactively go
visit our work,

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:40.240
we're not actually
closing that gap.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.340
So that's one problem.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:44.290
We also see something
else kind of interesting

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:49.410
happen as data science
teams try to close this gap.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.460
And so you know one
thing, as we said,

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.740
is that maybe the dashboards
fall by the wayside.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.720
Another thing that
can often happen

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.460
is that stakeholders
will get excited.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.730
They'll say, great, this
insight makes sense,

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:03.190
can you bring it to my medium.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:06.160
Can you copy and paste
those predictions

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.510
into my spreadsheet.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:10.570
Or can you send me a
screenshot of that plot

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.550
so I can put it in
my presentation.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:16.730
And that leads to a lot of
painful labor-intensive work.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.730
One of the interesting
things of the last couple

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:22.570
months that I got to work at
home with my wife, who is also

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:25.210
an R user, and one day
looking over her shoulder,

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.910
we kind of had this
exchange, where she was doing

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:29.440
exactly what I just described.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.510
She was taking the
results of some R code

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.150
and plugging them into a
spreadsheet for her boss.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:37.720
And me being the R studio fanboy
kind of said, why don't you

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:38.700
create a shiny app?

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.500
That'll be really awesome,
it'd save you all this time,

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.679
and it could be a big win
for your organization.

00:06:44.679 --> 00:06:49.390
And I wish I could have captured
the look on my wife's face.

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:52.150
And her response
here, she basically

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:53.950
said was, like,
my boss, Dave, has

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:55.450
been looking at the
same spreadsheet

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.550
every week for years.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.100
Any one who tries
to make changes

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.680
gets decimated, basically.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.330
And so she was kind of stuck
in this equally problematic

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:07.990
scenario--

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:11.200
I mean, at least her boss
was engaging with her work--

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.150
but it was in a
way that was really

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:14.860
killing her productivity.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.660
We often refer to
these as fire drills

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:19.330
where you have to answer
ad hoc questions that

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:22.390
require a lot of manual
labor-intensive work,

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:24.190
especially on the
formatting side

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:28.210
if you're copying and pasting
results into different mediums.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:31.890
And so to summarize kind
of the intro here briefly,

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:34.150
data science teams often
and can find themselves

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:37.780
trying to thread this
challenging needle, where

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:40.600
on the one hand, you
might put a lot of effort

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.580
into something really
awesome like a dashboard,

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:45.375
only does see that it kind
of falls by the wayside.

00:07:45.375 --> 00:07:47.250
On the other hand, you
spend all of your time

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:51.220
kind of in these manual
ad hoc fire drills.

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:54.160
Neither of those are great
ways to in the long run

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.270
close that gap between data
scientists and decision makers.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.150
But I'd like to
propose for you today

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:04.090
an alternative
solution that we've

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:07.810
found a lot of success using
ourselves here at RStudio.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:09.700
So what does this
solution entail?

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:13.630
Instead of creating a
dashboard, what we like to do

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:17.500
is create proactive
notifier or something

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.190
that is going to alert
me to the information

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:22.372
that I need to know.

00:08:22.372 --> 00:08:23.830
And in our case,
you know, we spend

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:25.980
most of our time
inside of our inboxes,

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:28.690
so that's where our alerts
goes-- is through email.

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.130
But we still want to
be able to do that

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:33.700
as data scientists writing code.

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:35.950
And so that's what we're
going to talk about today--

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:39.640
is how you can use a
Rich set of R packages

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:45.020
to allow you to create these
proactive notifications.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.410
And as I mentioned in kind
of with my wife's dilemma,

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:49.360
oftentimes, these
notifications need

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.700
to meet the expectations
of our stakeholders

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.080
for how they're formatted.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:55.120
So we'll look at
some new packages

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.660
that are available to help you
precisely format these results.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.470
And then finally, we don't want
to solve this dashboard fatigue

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:04.960
problem by introducing
another type of fatigue,

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.350
which would be email fatigue.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.840
And so we're going to talk
about how you can only send

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:13.270
these alerts on conditions.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:17.410
So that instead of
sending emails every day,

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:19.390
you can send emails
more specifically when

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:21.880
there's a problem at hand.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:24.370
And then finally, we've been
talking a lot about fatigue--

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:27.040
we don't want to introduce email
fatigue, dashboard fatigue.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:29.270
We also don't want to
introduce data science fatigue.

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:31.540
So we're going to talk
about how we can achieve

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:34.380
these proactive notifications
in an automated way

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:37.750
so that someone doesn't have
to click play every morning.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.440
The picture that
you're seeing here

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:42.540
is one of my favorite
examples of this workflow.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:44.480
And one of my
roles at RStudio is

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:47.540
to keep track of all the
different R packages that we

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:49.100
build and maintain.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:50.900
For some of you on
the line, R packages

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:52.540
might be a newer thing.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:57.200
So briefly, they're just
basically extensions

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:58.498
of the R ecosystem.

00:09:58.498 --> 00:10:01.040
They're sort of the bread and
butter of how data science gets

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:02.720
done in R. And they're also--

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:04.130
one of the main
things that we're

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:06.777
responsible for at RStudio
is creating these different R

00:10:06.777 --> 00:10:07.550
packages.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:12.170
And so every day, we go and we
build all of these packages,

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:16.110
and we want to know if
any of those builds fail.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:18.470
Especially if it's a
really popular package,

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:21.530
we don't want to get a
whole bunch of emails

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:23.270
from users saying,
hey, this package

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:25.190
isn't available or doesn't work.

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:28.370
We kind of want to stay ahead
of those types of problems.

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:30.680
And so what the data scientist
named Greg and I came up

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:34.700
with is this workflow, where
everyday, we run these builds.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:36.980
And then if there's a
problem, and critically,

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:39.410
only if there's a problem,
we get these types

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:42.980
of targeted emails that tell
me exactly what the problem was

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.780
and allow me to then
take further action.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.510
And an example of that
email from my inbox

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:52.140
is what you're
seeing on the screen.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.280
We're not the only ones
who are kind of using

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:55.720
this pattern successfully.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:58.450
So I just want to tell you a
few quick stories before we

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:01.450
get into the weeds
of writing the code

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:04.990
one of my favorite stories is
from a large infrastructure

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:06.500
as a service provider.

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:09.820
So a company that maintains
thousands, if not hundreds

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:12.160
of thousands of servers.

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:15.100
And what they use
this workflow to do

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:17.780
was track their hardware uptime.

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:20.020
Then if they saw
a problem arising,

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:22.270
they could send a
proactive alert out

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.330
to their vendors in the
context of their support level

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:27.280
agreement or SLA.

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:29.320
And what this team was
really excited about is

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.550
before adopting this workflow,
they had done this type of task

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:33.730
in Jira.

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:36.940
And anytime they onboarding
a new vendor or they modify

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:39.340
their SLA, they had
to manually click

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:41.170
through a whole bunch
of steps of Jira

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:43.512
to accomplish this task.

00:11:43.512 --> 00:11:44.970
The great thing
about this workflow

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:47.950
is that it's all base on
code, so it's really easy

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:52.000
to reuse these different
rules and different R Markdown

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:53.920
documents that are
going to write.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:56.360
And so this allowed the
team to easily cut down

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:59.320
their onboarding time.

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:03.640
Another really awesome story of
this type of technology applied

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:06.970
was in a high tech manufacturer
people ops department-- so kind

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:08.730
of an HR group.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:10.300
And they had this
challenging task,

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.060
where they needed to
notify division managers

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:16.000
about employee
overtime problems.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.820
So say an employee
worked more overtime

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.550
than they were allowed-- they
wanted to send out an alert.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:25.220
Before they had a dashboard,
and what they found was

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:28.180
there are 30 different division
managers we're not reliably

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.890
going to that
dashboard every day

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:32.410
to check if there was problems.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:35.170
So they adopted this
workflow to instead send out

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:38.500
an email to a manager
only if there was actually

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:40.750
a problem with a specific
employee's over time

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:41.940
submission.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:44.110
And they actually won
an award of excellence

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:46.570
for adopting this
workflow, which

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:49.780
I think is really exciting,
because it's ultimately

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:52.660
a pretty simple concept that
we're talking about here.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.007
And so if they're able
to win excellence awards,

00:12:55.007 --> 00:12:57.340
I think that's going to be
accessible to a lot of teams,

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:59.050
as well.

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:01.307
So let's get into the nuts
and bolts a little bit

00:13:01.307 --> 00:13:03.390
to set the stage for Rich,
who's going to walk you

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:04.405
through the code here.

00:13:04.405 --> 00:13:06.780
I just want to talk about the
different tools in our tool

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:10.470
belt that are going to allow
us to apply this workflow.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:12.870
And so the first
thing that I mentioned

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:15.440
is that we're going
to be crafting emails.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:18.570
And to do that, we're
going to use to R packages.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:20.070
The first one is R Markdown.

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:21.870
If you're not familiar
with R Markdown,

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:25.050
it's a package that
allows you to combine code

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:27.860
and text to create documents--

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:32.220
beautifully kind of
customizable documents.

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:34.080
And so historically,
R Markdown has

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.590
been used to create PDFs
files or HTML files.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:41.340
But in our case, what we
want to use R Markdown for

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:44.340
is creating the actual
body of an email.

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:47.880
It turns out an email's body
is kind of unique and different

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.610
from other types
of output formats.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:53.310
And so to help kind
of adjust R Markdown

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.140
to create the body
of an email, we've

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:57.660
introduced a new
package called Blastula.

00:13:57.660 --> 00:13:59.760
And so that's what Blastula
is really good at--

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:03.650
is helping you craft
the body of your email.

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:06.040
We also mentioned
that often, when

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:09.070
you implement these
types of workflows,

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:11.860
you need to format results
in a very specific way.

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:15.130
And because of kind of the
legacy of spreadsheets,

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:18.120
that very specific format
tends to be a table.

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:20.530
And so we're going to spend
a little time walking you

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:24.520
through GT, which is a package
that allows you to have

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:27.490
a lot of control over
the types of tables

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:30.250
that you can produce
from R, and it's also

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:32.980
compatible with
Blastula and R Markdown.

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:35.830
You can embed those tables
right into the email,

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:39.520
like you saw in my example.

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:41.272
So those are kind
of the packages.

00:14:41.272 --> 00:14:42.980
And then we get to
what I think is really

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:45.440
the heart of this
whole solution, which

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:49.250
is how we can send these types
of notifications in a more

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:52.200
dynamic way and on a condition.

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.260
And the amazing thing
about writing code

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:57.720
is that our answer here for
what can our condition be

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.060
is really endless.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.430
With code, the
answer is always yes.

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:03.760
That is really true here.

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:05.690
Your condition
might be something

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:07.619
like anomaly detection.

00:15:07.619 --> 00:15:10.190
It might be a complex
model that allows

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:13.310
you to determine dynamic
thresholds with uncertainty

00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:14.359
levels.

00:15:14.359 --> 00:15:16.970
In our case, it's going to be
just a simple set of rules.

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:21.050
But no matter what it is,
the actual implementation

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:23.810
of sending a notification
on a condition

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:27.260
boils down to a really simple
if statement that I think

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:30.875
is kind of at the heart of how
powerful this workflow can be.

00:15:30.875 --> 00:15:32.250
And then finally,
I mentioned you

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:34.850
know we don't want to
introduce data science fatigue,

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:37.850
so we don't want to use
these tools then still end up

00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:40.880
in a world where everyday
we have to press Play

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:44.478
on rendering these reports
and sending out these alerts.

00:15:44.478 --> 00:15:46.520
And so we're going to talk
a little bit about how

00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:48.560
to automate this workflow.

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:50.360
Now there's a lot
of open source ways

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:52.850
that you could go
about doing this.

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:54.560
You could use
something as simple

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:58.400
as Crontab to manage the
execution of these reports.

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:01.220
Or perhaps you could use
a more advanced scheduler,

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:02.602
something like Airflow.

00:16:02.602 --> 00:16:05.060
But our case, we're going to
look at RStudio Connect, which

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:09.170
is a professional product
that RStudio kind of sells.

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:12.740
And what RStudio
Connect does really well

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:15.770
is it takes care of some of
the intricacies of putting R

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:16.780
into production.

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:19.400
So you don't have to worry
about the different packages

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:20.850
that your report might need.

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:23.570
You don't have to worry
about login failures,

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:27.680
about authenticating users,
about securely sending email,

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:29.490
or scaling out the system.

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:33.080
So it's a really powerful way to
adopt a lot of these automation

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:35.840
features without having to
reinvent them all yourselves.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:38.960
So that's what we're
going to show you today.

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:40.460
But the key to all
of this, I think,

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:42.293
is that the code that
we're going to look at

00:16:42.293 --> 00:16:44.780
is actually really fun to write.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:47.600
And that's important, because
doing something that's fun

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:51.460
is obviously a good incentive
to do it in the first place,

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:54.050
but also makes it easier to
share these types of workflows

00:16:54.050 --> 00:16:56.570
amongst your organization
and to other people

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:59.030
kind of adopt them, as
well, which is critical

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:03.410
if you want to have a critical
mass around this new type

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:05.390
of workflow.

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:07.550
So to set the stage
here Rich, this

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:09.710
is what we're going to
walk through in our demo.

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:12.160
We're going to start
with some fake data.

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:14.450
And then we're going
to go from that data

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:17.600
to a set of key performance
indicators or KPIs that

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:19.100
are going to serve
as our threshold

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:21.580
for sending alert notifications.

00:17:21.580 --> 00:17:23.690
In our case, those are
going to be simple rules.

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:25.773
In your case, that's really
where the data science

00:17:25.773 --> 00:17:27.410
magic applies.

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:29.410
And so you might
apply some magic

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:31.658
and give us the benefit
of the doubt here.

00:17:31.658 --> 00:17:33.200
And then what we're
going to show you

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:34.810
is kind of a natural first step.

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:36.470
Once we have that
data and those KPIs,

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:38.180
we're going to
build a dashboard.

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:40.430
And don't get me wrong--

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:42.080
the point of today's
webinar is not

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:44.380
to say that dashboards
should go away.

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:46.880
Dashboards serve a
really important purpose

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:49.850
in helping create mutual
understanding between you

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:51.020
and your stakeholders.

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:53.720
It's a great way,
especially in a meeting,

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:57.380
to play around with the
analysis and allow stakeholders

00:17:57.380 --> 00:18:00.170
to bring their domain
expertise to the table

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:02.780
and maybe informing what
thresholds or KPI should

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:05.270
be base on their experience.

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:08.670
But it's not a great
place to stop over time.

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:11.270
And so what we're going to do
once we have our dashboard is

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:15.470
show you how pretty much
the same code can be adapted

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:16.850
with the packages I mentioned--

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:18.920
Blastula, R Markdown, and GT--

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:20.798
to create these
automated emails.

00:18:20.798 --> 00:18:23.090
Them we're going to put them
into production on RStudio

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:25.580
Connect and show you
