00:36:42.230 --> 00:36:44.450
So one of the first
questions that I

00:36:44.450 --> 00:36:47.750
saw that I thought was really
interesting was a question that

00:36:47.750 --> 00:36:52.170
asked, what types of things
can you embed in the emails?

00:36:52.170 --> 00:36:57.860
So we demonstrated embedding
ggplots and tables.

00:36:57.860 --> 00:36:59.480
This particular
participant asked

00:36:59.480 --> 00:37:02.320
about interactive hml widgets.

00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:05.558
And so Rich, maybe if you could
give a shot at answering that.

00:37:05.558 --> 00:37:06.350
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:37:06.350 --> 00:37:08.240
Well, it's a little
bit of a letdown,

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:11.780
but not really, because
email clients do not like

00:37:11.780 --> 00:37:13.850
any JavaScript in their email.

00:37:13.850 --> 00:37:16.740
They tend to strip those away.

00:37:16.740 --> 00:37:18.338
So that is kind of out.

00:37:18.338 --> 00:37:19.880
The most interactive
you can probably

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:24.860
get is simple HTML elements
and links to see it

00:37:24.860 --> 00:37:28.210
in a full featured app, is what
people are doing with email.

00:37:28.210 --> 00:37:30.910
So interactive bits
like HTML widgets

00:37:30.910 --> 00:37:38.020
are not possible until email
gets beyond where it is now.

00:37:38.020 --> 00:37:41.230
Right now, it's very
stripped down because

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:44.510
of the security model of email.

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:50.072
So that's a bummer answer,
but basically don't try it.

00:37:50.072 --> 00:37:52.430
SEAN LOPP: Yeah.

00:37:52.430 --> 00:37:56.255
A follow on question
was, we think

00:37:56.255 --> 00:37:59.360
you showed Apple Mail,
which got some applause

00:37:59.360 --> 00:38:00.472
from the audience, Rich.

00:38:00.472 --> 00:38:01.180
RICH IANNONE: Oh!

00:38:01.180 --> 00:38:02.680
SEAN LOPP: But we'll
throw out there

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:05.630
that the approach has
been validated and tested

00:38:05.630 --> 00:38:07.580
on a whole bunch
of email clients.

00:38:07.580 --> 00:38:10.610
So for those formats
that are supported,

00:38:10.610 --> 00:38:15.890
you'll be able to use Outlook,
Gmail, whatever on your phone

00:38:15.890 --> 00:38:17.360
or on a laptop.

00:38:17.360 --> 00:38:18.860
RICH IANNONE: I'll
throw it in there

00:38:18.860 --> 00:38:23.870
that we made a very large
effort in our QA process

00:38:23.870 --> 00:38:26.290
to test as many email
clients as possible,

00:38:26.290 --> 00:38:29.390
but arrange sending
types with Blastula

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:34.380
through Connect, just sending
through your own SMTP server,

00:38:34.380 --> 00:38:36.200
like via Gmail.

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:39.240
We're actually using a service
called Litmus, which will--

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:42.080
you send them the email
and they will test it

00:38:42.080 --> 00:38:45.440
on 50 plus email clients.

00:38:45.440 --> 00:38:47.690
And we can see the results
and if things break.

00:38:47.690 --> 00:38:49.640
So we were able to
fix a ton of problems.

00:38:49.640 --> 00:38:53.870
And more importantly to
ensure that your email will

00:38:53.870 --> 00:38:56.510
be delivered without
being mangled,

00:38:56.510 --> 00:38:58.885
which is always a good thing.

00:38:58.885 --> 00:39:00.250
SEAN LOPP: Awesome.

00:39:00.250 --> 00:39:04.270
I'll take another group of
the questions here Rich,

00:39:04.270 --> 00:39:10.655
was what if you don't want
to use RStudio connect?

00:39:10.655 --> 00:39:13.930
So I can take a stab
at answering that.

00:39:13.930 --> 00:39:17.470
The key to all of this,
that's two parts that you

00:39:17.470 --> 00:39:19.060
would need to implement.

00:39:19.060 --> 00:39:23.843
One is a way to, on a schedule,
render an R Markdown document.

00:39:23.843 --> 00:39:25.760
And there's lots of
different ways to do that.

00:39:25.760 --> 00:39:27.640
You can use something
like Cron, you

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:30.640
can use something more
sophisticated like Airflow,

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:34.315
or you can manually click
Render on a report every day.

00:39:34.315 --> 00:39:36.680
So there's lots
of options there.

00:39:36.680 --> 00:39:40.480
The second bit is that you
need to use a service that

00:39:40.480 --> 00:39:44.740
will send the rendered email.

00:39:44.740 --> 00:39:46.930
And so, Rich mentioned
briefly, and maybe you

00:39:46.930 --> 00:39:49.870
can expand upon this,
Rich But Blastula also

00:39:49.870 --> 00:39:55.060
has functions for integrating
with non-RStudio Connect email

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:57.825
senders, like an SMTP service.

00:39:57.825 --> 00:39:59.200
RICH IANNONE:
Yeah, that's right.

00:39:59.200 --> 00:40:02.460
So you can use
Gmail's SMTP service.

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:06.730
You can use Microsoft's
Outlook one, the online one.

00:40:06.730 --> 00:40:10.360
And you can also hook up any
of the ones like SMTP2GO,

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:12.970
which is a really nice one.

00:40:12.970 --> 00:40:14.700
The problem is, it's
not really viable.

00:40:14.700 --> 00:40:16.478
I found Connect is rock solid.

00:40:16.478 --> 00:40:17.520
Always sends your emails.

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:21.110
Sometimes you get throttled
with those other services.

00:40:21.110 --> 00:40:23.500
So that's a little
bit of uncertainty

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:25.990
you may have to deal with.

00:40:25.990 --> 00:40:28.653
But it's possible.

00:40:28.653 --> 00:40:29.320
SEAN LOPP: Yeah.

00:40:29.320 --> 00:40:30.945
The other thing I'll
add to that, Rich,

00:40:30.945 --> 00:40:34.000
that you will want to be
a little bit careful of,

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:37.480
is that when you're sending
these types of notifications,

00:40:37.480 --> 00:40:41.900
you just need to watch out
for impersonation issues.

00:40:41.900 --> 00:40:44.380
So one of the things that
we've worked hard on Connect

00:40:44.380 --> 00:40:48.070
is to make sure that when you
send an email, it's coming,

00:40:48.070 --> 00:40:49.810
even though it's an
automated system,

00:40:49.810 --> 00:40:52.720
it's coming from a
reputable address

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:56.090
and then going to the people
that are designated to see it.

00:40:56.090 --> 00:40:58.000
And so there are some
security ramifications

00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:01.000
when you get into the realm
of automating notifications.

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:03.670
You just want to be
careful of if you're

00:41:03.670 --> 00:41:05.890
integrating with one of
those other options yourself.

00:41:09.730 --> 00:41:10.810
Let's see.

00:41:10.810 --> 00:41:13.430
In terms of other
interesting questions,

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:15.520
someone had asked a
pretty interesting one,

00:41:15.520 --> 00:41:17.560
which is, do you have to use R?

00:41:17.560 --> 00:41:21.910
Or is there a way to incorporate
other languages like Python

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:23.440
into these types of workflows?

00:41:25.588 --> 00:41:27.130
RICH IANNONE: I'm
sure there's a way.

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:32.020
But the thing you're missing
is Blastula or training

00:41:32.020 --> 00:41:34.210
the email message body.

00:41:34.210 --> 00:41:37.630
And Python does have--

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:38.680
I've looked around.

00:41:38.680 --> 00:41:42.460
And I've looked for
other Python libraries

00:41:42.460 --> 00:41:45.943
that do this sort of
thing, like create a email.

00:41:45.943 --> 00:41:48.360
And certainly, you can send
an email great through Python.

00:41:48.360 --> 00:41:50.318
But the hard part is
creating the message body.

00:41:50.318 --> 00:41:54.370
And the even harder part is
creating the HTML message body,

00:41:54.370 --> 00:41:57.290
which is what Blastula does.

00:41:57.290 --> 00:41:58.980
So I think it's possible.

00:41:58.980 --> 00:42:00.730
You just have to do a
lot of upfront work.

00:42:00.730 --> 00:42:05.350
You basically have to
reproduce Blastula in Python

00:42:05.350 --> 00:42:07.330
to get that going.

00:42:07.330 --> 00:42:08.710
I'd love to find
out if there are

00:42:08.710 --> 00:42:11.260
any other packages or sorry--
libraries in Python that

00:42:11.260 --> 00:42:13.020
actually do what Blastula does.

00:42:13.020 --> 00:42:15.660
But in my search I
couldn't find any.

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:19.690
SEAN LOPP: I'll add
to that, Rich, one way

00:42:19.690 --> 00:42:22.120
that you could combine
these two together

00:42:22.120 --> 00:42:24.640
is actually within R Markdown.

00:42:24.640 --> 00:42:28.420
So it's a bit of a
misnomer, but R Markdown

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:31.300
allows you to combine a whole
bunch different languages.

00:42:31.300 --> 00:42:33.970
So while it's an R
Markdown document,

00:42:33.970 --> 00:42:37.600
you can certainly have
Python code chunks commingled

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:41.590
with R code chunks, commingled
with bash code chunks,

00:42:41.590 --> 00:42:43.190
if you want it, or SQL.

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:46.030
So all those different languages
can live inside the R Markdown

00:42:46.030 --> 00:42:47.110
document.

00:42:47.110 --> 00:42:52.180
And then R, in that
type of workflow,

00:42:52.180 --> 00:42:54.490
just becomes a
facilitator for sending

00:42:54.490 --> 00:42:59.278
the email using Rich's
awesome work on Blastula.

00:42:59.278 --> 00:43:00.070
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:43:00.070 --> 00:43:01.320
I'm glad you brought that
up, because yeah, definitely

00:43:01.320 --> 00:43:03.630
all that other stuff,
you can do in R Markdown.

00:43:03.630 --> 00:43:06.785
It has a lot of
engines available,

00:43:06.785 --> 00:43:08.970
but the last piece
is kind of critical.

00:43:12.760 --> 00:43:15.370
SEAN LOPP: So this is an
interesting question, Rich,

00:43:15.370 --> 00:43:20.350
on a little bit more
philosophical, I guess.

00:43:20.350 --> 00:43:24.130
The person asks, how is
this approach influencing

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:29.050
your thinking around dashboards
and when you would use

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:32.188
a dashboard and
when you wouldn't?

00:43:32.188 --> 00:43:32.980
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:36.390
A dashboard, it's
still pretty essential.

00:43:36.390 --> 00:43:39.080
But I wouldn't use it as
the way to notify people

00:43:39.080 --> 00:43:43.470
of the important, because it
involves diligence and seeing

00:43:43.470 --> 00:43:46.940
the dashboards
daily, or whichever

00:43:46.940 --> 00:43:50.900
frequency is required to be
ahead of important issues.

00:43:50.900 --> 00:43:53.610
So a dashboard is part
of the whole thing,

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:57.050
like you can go to a dashboard
once you get notified,

00:43:57.050 --> 00:44:01.700
and then get the history of
what led to certain problems up

00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:04.028
to the present day or whatever.

00:44:04.028 --> 00:44:05.570
It's not a replacement
for dashboard.

00:44:05.570 --> 00:44:11.970
It's basically adds to the
whole stack of analytics.

00:44:11.970 --> 00:44:15.860
SEAN LOPP: One thing I'll
echo there that also addresses

00:44:15.860 --> 00:44:18.500
another question
that was asked, we

00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:20.090
touched on this very briefly.

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:23.990
But a common pattern in
those emails that you said,

00:44:23.990 --> 00:44:28.110
is to dynamically generate
a link back to a dashboard.

00:44:28.110 --> 00:44:30.470
And so something that
we do a lot at RStudio,

00:44:30.470 --> 00:44:32.750
and the example I
mentioned at the beginning,

00:44:32.750 --> 00:44:34.710
I'll get an email
that says, hey,

00:44:34.710 --> 00:44:36.720
these packages failed to build.

00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:39.080
And then it includes
a link to a dashboard

00:44:39.080 --> 00:44:43.550
where I can explore the
error codes and the duration

00:44:43.550 --> 00:44:46.920
of the job and all sorts of
more interactive questions.

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:49.840
So dashboards aren't
out of the picture.

00:44:49.840 --> 00:44:52.550
They just, as Rich that,
aren't the mechanism

00:44:52.550 --> 00:44:55.138
for doing the notification.

00:44:55.138 --> 00:44:56.045
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:44:56.045 --> 00:44:56.545
Exactly.

00:44:59.360 --> 00:45:01.640
SEAN LOPP: There
was a question here.

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:03.830
Instead of hard
coding the thresholds,

00:45:03.830 --> 00:45:06.280
they can be dynamic, right?

00:45:06.280 --> 00:45:06.780
Yes.

00:45:06.780 --> 00:45:07.210
RICH IANNONE: Absolutely.

00:45:07.210 --> 00:45:08.085
SEAN LOPP: Of course.

00:45:08.085 --> 00:45:10.010
RICH IANNONE: This is
basically your code.

00:45:10.010 --> 00:45:12.230
I just basically whipped
something up together,

00:45:12.230 --> 00:45:15.530
very unsophisticated,
whether it's below or above,

00:45:15.530 --> 00:45:17.960
you can definitely pull in
data, generate the thresholds

00:45:17.960 --> 00:45:20.297
dynamically, compare.

00:45:20.297 --> 00:45:21.630
There's lots of ways to do this.

00:45:21.630 --> 00:45:24.200
You can use packages
like anomaly detection,

00:45:24.200 --> 00:45:29.840
or you can use the forecast
package to do certain things.

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:32.570
Essentially, what you want to
do is you want to get to a place

00:45:32.570 --> 00:45:34.820
where you can make a final
decision on whether to send

00:45:34.820 --> 00:45:38.230
the email or not and then
what to put in that email.

00:45:38.230 --> 00:45:41.340
And you may have different
versions of email.

00:45:41.340 --> 00:45:43.780
You may select between
different types,

00:45:43.780 --> 00:45:47.690
or you may be more sophisticated
in the dynamic content you

00:45:47.690 --> 00:45:51.830
include like the subject line
or even the content of email,

00:45:51.830 --> 00:45:53.840
because the variables
are transmitted

00:45:53.840 --> 00:45:56.300
from the main document to
the sub document, which

00:45:56.300 --> 00:45:58.740
is kind of cool.

00:45:58.740 --> 00:45:59.240
So yeah.

00:45:59.240 --> 00:46:03.500
Basically, because this is code,
you can do whatever you want.

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:06.080
You can go nuts, essentially.

00:46:06.080 --> 00:46:07.460
SEAN LOPP: Yeah.

00:46:07.460 --> 00:46:15.680
A kind of follow on question
was, maybe as a data scientist,

00:46:15.680 --> 00:46:17.415
I don't know the
triggers or thresholds.

00:46:17.415 --> 00:46:19.040
And so the question
was, does it always

00:46:19.040 --> 00:46:21.290
fall on the data science
team to set them?

00:46:21.290 --> 00:46:22.820
Or is there some
way for decision

00:46:22.820 --> 00:46:26.258
makers to influence
the thresholds?

00:46:26.258 --> 00:46:27.050
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:46:27.050 --> 00:46:30.600
That sort of thing, I think it's
a conversation between the data

00:46:30.600 --> 00:46:36.694
team and the decision makers in
their organization, for sure.

00:46:36.694 --> 00:46:38.236
You make a set of
of recommendations.

00:46:38.236 --> 00:46:43.080
You base change them on a weekly
basis or some other frequency.

00:46:43.080 --> 00:46:44.327
They may be dynamic.

00:46:44.327 --> 00:46:46.410
They may just be a system
which creates thresholds

00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:50.550
if values are changing fast and
there's seasonality or things

00:46:50.550 --> 00:46:51.472
like that.

00:46:51.472 --> 00:46:53.180
So it really depends
on your organization

00:46:53.180 --> 00:46:56.220
but essentially, it should
be transparent, I think,

00:46:56.220 --> 00:46:58.930
because decision makers are
getting the notification.

00:46:58.930 --> 00:47:02.060
They probably should know why.

00:47:02.060 --> 00:47:03.170
Yeah.

00:47:03.170 --> 00:47:06.760
Basically, it's a conversation.

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:07.850
SEAN LOPP: Yep.

00:47:07.850 --> 00:47:12.910
And the nice thing about code
is that it's not a black box.

00:47:12.910 --> 00:47:14.690
It's very inspectable,
and it gives you

00:47:14.690 --> 00:47:16.680
the full flexibility there.

00:47:16.680 --> 00:47:18.163
So you're not locked in.

00:47:18.163 --> 00:47:20.330
It's not like R Studio's
deciding what the threshold

00:47:20.330 --> 00:47:21.870
should be for you.

00:47:21.870 --> 00:47:25.048
You get all the options there.

00:47:25.048 --> 00:47:25.840
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:28.152
SEAN LOPP: And
that kind of leads

00:47:28.152 --> 00:47:30.110
to another question that
came up that I thought

00:47:30.110 --> 00:47:32.180
was really awesome.

00:47:32.180 --> 00:47:36.500
We talked a lot in this webinar
about creating notifications

00:47:36.500 --> 00:47:39.530
to bridge the gap between the
data scientists and decision

00:47:39.530 --> 00:47:44.060
makers, but this person
asked, could the same workflow

00:47:44.060 --> 00:47:50.170
apply to notify the data science
team of data quality issues?

00:47:50.170 --> 00:47:50.670
Absolutely.

00:47:50.670 --> 00:47:51.570
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:47:51.570 --> 00:47:53.100
Yeah, definitely.

00:47:53.100 --> 00:47:53.600
Yeah.

00:47:53.600 --> 00:47:58.670
I mean, it's totally up to you
who the email's directed to.

00:47:58.670 --> 00:48:03.470
I would set up diagnostic emails
to yourself, if important.

00:48:03.470 --> 00:48:10.277
If certain data is suspect
based on some data validation

00:48:10.277 --> 00:48:11.110
rules that you have.

00:48:11.110 --> 00:48:12.590
I would want to know about it.

00:48:12.590 --> 00:48:15.620
And then I would want
to have the engineering

00:48:15.620 --> 00:48:18.660
know about it too, to get
to the root cause of things.

00:48:18.660 --> 00:48:19.790
So absolutely, yeah.

00:48:19.790 --> 00:48:23.410
Definitely send yourself
these notifications.

00:48:23.410 --> 00:48:23.910
Yeah.

00:48:23.910 --> 00:48:26.480
That's my pitch.

00:48:26.480 --> 00:48:29.040
SEAN LOPP: So those were
some high level questions.

00:48:29.040 --> 00:48:33.360
There's a few more in
the weeds questions

00:48:33.360 --> 00:48:36.540
that we'll address
here at the end.

00:48:36.540 --> 00:48:39.210
One of them is from someone
who's already playing around

00:48:39.210 --> 00:48:39.930
with the code.

00:48:39.930 --> 00:48:42.540
So kudos to you.

00:48:42.540 --> 00:48:45.900
And they've hit
a stumbling block

00:48:45.900 --> 00:48:49.230
that is maybe a little
bit common as you're

00:48:49.230 --> 00:48:51.290
learning this workflow.

00:48:51.290 --> 00:48:53.520
And rather than reading
the question verbatim,

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:56.220
Rich, I'll basically diagnose
it and maybe you could even

00:48:56.220 --> 00:48:58.470
show the workflow here.

00:48:58.470 --> 00:49:00.150
But essentially,
what they did was

00:49:00.150 --> 00:49:03.672
they rendered the child document
instead of the main document.

00:49:03.672 --> 00:49:04.380
RICH IANNONE: Ah.

00:49:04.380 --> 00:49:06.980
SEAN LOPP: So can you talk a
little bit about which document

00:49:06.980 --> 00:49:08.130
to render and where?

00:49:08.130 --> 00:49:08.922
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:49:08.922 --> 00:49:10.497
So this is a trap
I always fall into,

00:49:10.497 --> 00:49:13.080
because it doesn't stop you from
rendering the child document.

00:49:13.080 --> 00:49:14.460
So it's always
the main document.

00:49:14.460 --> 00:49:16.320
So if you render
the child document,

00:49:16.320 --> 00:49:17.830
nothing good comes from that.

00:49:17.830 --> 00:49:18.330
Yeah.

00:49:18.330 --> 00:49:19.880
There should probably
be a warning somewhere,

00:49:19.880 --> 00:49:20.960
but there's nowhere to put it.

00:49:20.960 --> 00:49:23.210
But essentially, it all
comes from the main document.

00:49:23.210 --> 00:49:24.760
And that gives you two previews.

00:49:24.760 --> 00:49:26.760
So basically, the thing
you want to ask yourself

00:49:26.760 --> 00:49:28.320
is, am I seeing two previews?

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:30.190
And if not, I probably
did something wrong.

00:49:30.190 --> 00:49:35.460
So always hit knit on the main
document, and then you'll A-OK.

00:49:35.460 --> 00:49:37.895
Otherwise, it won't
harm anything,

00:49:37.895 --> 00:49:39.520
it just won't give
you anything useful.

00:49:39.520 --> 00:49:42.503
It'll probably just give
you something not great.

00:49:42.503 --> 00:49:43.170
SEAN LOPP: Yeah.

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:44.880
The error messages
that you'll tend

00:49:44.880 --> 00:49:48.120
to see if you accidentally
that the child document are

00:49:48.120 --> 00:49:50.257
that variables are undefined.

00:49:50.257 --> 00:49:51.840
And that's because
those variables are

00:49:51.840 --> 00:49:53.625
defined in your main document.

00:49:53.625 --> 00:49:54.750
RICH IANNONE: That's right.

00:49:54.750 --> 00:49:57.292
SEAN LOPP: Now, of course you
could duplicate all of the code

00:49:57.292 --> 00:50:00.570
into the child document to have
a child document that's also

00:50:00.570 --> 00:50:04.170
standalone, but then you've
needlessly copied and pasted

00:50:04.170 --> 00:50:06.828
code, which is something that
we're always trying to avoid.

00:50:06.828 --> 00:50:07.620
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:50:07.620 --> 00:50:11.550
Try to keep dry, even in your
email message bodies or your R

00:50:11.550 --> 00:50:13.280
Markdown documents.

00:50:13.280 --> 00:50:16.050
So people that know what dry
means, don't repeat yourself.

00:50:16.050 --> 00:50:19.290
Try as much as possible to
have one place for code.

00:50:19.290 --> 00:50:21.680
And that's the great thing
about these sub-documents,

00:50:21.680 --> 00:50:24.120
taking results from
the main document.

00:50:24.120 --> 00:50:26.100
You don't have to
re-write code and worry

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:29.811
about being in sync with
between the documents.

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:34.290
SEAN LOPP: Excellent.

00:50:34.290 --> 00:50:39.810
And then, just for the
sake of being in the weeds

00:50:39.810 --> 00:50:43.530
with our audience here, maybe
one final question for you,

00:50:43.530 --> 00:50:44.610
Rich.

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:49.200
This one was related to GT,
which we spoke on briefly.

00:50:49.200 --> 00:50:54.000
And they said, how would
you apply the same row

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:57.460
rules across multiple columns?

00:50:57.460 --> 00:51:00.080
And maybe you can just use
this as an opportunity, Rich,

00:51:00.080 --> 00:51:05.220
to speak a little bit more
towards what the philosophy is

00:51:05.220 --> 00:51:08.800
behind writing GT code and
doing the types of highlighting

00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:10.368
that you demo-ed today.

00:51:10.368 --> 00:51:11.160
RICH IANNONE: Yeah.

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:13.077
Basically, what you're
doing with highlighting

00:51:13.077 --> 00:51:15.180
is you're using tab
style, and you're

00:51:15.180 --> 00:51:19.110
specifying either the whole
row, the whole column,

00:51:19.110 --> 00:51:21.970
or an individual cell with
the locations argument.

00:51:21.970 --> 00:51:23.970
So it's basically like
you're on an Excel sheet,

00:51:23.970 --> 00:51:26.400
and you're clicking
something to highlight it,

00:51:26.400 --> 00:51:28.920
and then you're doing
this bit of styling, which

00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:31.080
could be changing
the text, changing

00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:34.085
the background of
the cell, whatever.

00:51:34.085 --> 00:51:35.460
How I would apply
the same thing,

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:37.230
I would just use
the columns argument

00:51:37.230 --> 00:51:40.710
and specify multiple
columns within bars.

00:51:40.710 --> 00:51:42.602
That's how you normally do that.

00:51:42.602 --> 00:51:43.560
I would try that first.

00:51:43.560 --> 00:51:46.420
If it doesn't work, then
it might be something

00:51:46.420 --> 00:51:49.390
with a little bit more to it.

00:51:49.390 --> 00:51:52.380
And if the question
is super important,

00:51:52.380 --> 00:51:54.540
I would definitely ask
it in RStudio Community

00:51:54.540 --> 00:52:02.315
or even inside the GT issues
page in the repository.

00:52:02.315 --> 00:52:03.190
SEAN LOPP: Excellent.

00:52:03.190 --> 00:52:03.700
Yeah.

00:52:03.700 --> 00:52:07.810
So I think that's a
good stopping point.

00:52:07.810 --> 00:52:11.700
It allows us a chance to
